// What a character is worth as a number, when it is one.
//
// `"٣".parse_int()` fails, and `٣` is a three. So is `३`, `๓`, `三` and
// `Ⅲ`. A form that accepts a number, a parser reading somebody else's
// data, or a validator deciding whether a field is numeric all have to
// know which characters are digits and what they are worth, and "between
// `0` and `9`" is the answer for one alphabet out of a hundred.
//
// ## Three different questions
//
//   - **Decimal digit** — part of a positional decimal system, so `٣` in
//     `١٢٣` means three hundreds-tens-units the same way `3` does. This is
//     the only one a number parser should accept.
//   - **Digit** — looks like a digit and is not positional: `③`, `³`.
//   - **Numeric** — has a value at all: `Ⅷ` is eight, `½` is a half,
//     `万` is ten thousand.
//
// The three are nested, and a validator that asks the widest question
// accepts `½` as a digit. `is_decimal_digit` is almost always the one
// meant.
//
// ## Values are fractions
//
// A half, three quarters and a sixteenth are all Unicode numbers, so the
// value is a numerator and a denominator rather than an integer. Some are
// very large — a Han character for ten thousand times ten thousand — and
// the numerator is an `i64` for that reason.





/// Four numbers per row: the code point, what kind of numeric it is
/// (3 a decimal digit, 2 a digit, 1 a number), and the value as a
/// numerator and a denominator, because a half and three quarters are
/// both Unicode numbers and neither is an integer.
///
/// The Han numerals come from `Unihan_NumericValues.txt` rather than
/// `UnicodeData.txt`, which does not carry them: a table built from
/// only the one file answers no to whether a Han three is a number.
/// The Vietnamese, Zhuang and Tay readings are left out, being values
/// a character has in one tradition rather than as itself.
